A||N||66||fropuff||What is going on here?||||8||
Z||000000||fropuff||08-26-2000||09:46 PM||||Hey, while my brother and I were taking a little cruise in my '86 GT (about 10-15 miles), I noticed that my new theremostat wasn't reading.  Baffled, we took it back home.  While we were pulling into the garage, the oil pressure gauge dropped suddenly into the red zone and the light came on.  The RPMs also went way down, like the car was starting to stall.  We shut it off, and I popped the deck lid.  When I opened it, I could feel the heat rushing out.  I couldn't touch the deck lid grilles (vents), which is unusual for me only going about 15 miles.  I also couldn't put my hand on the top of the block, which is very unusual.  Usually, it is cool for the shorter trips.  The only time is was warm (which I could still place my hand on top) was when I drove it home from getting it, which was about 150 miles.  The deck lid was also hot.  What's causing this?  I changed the oil and filter about 100 miles ago, and the thermostat 25 miles ago.  What is going on here?  Thanks a lot for the help.  I just can't figure it out, and I don't want to take it out again 'till I fix it.  Thanks again.||||reg||8
Z||000001||fiero56||08-26-2000||09:51 PM||||I would venture to say that this new thermostat didn't open.<P>Get a pot of water, put it on the stove, turn on burner, put a thermometer in it, toss the thermostat in, and look to see what temperature the 'stat opens.<P>I have several theories about high temp, oil pressure loss, etc, but don't want to give you a heart attack before we find out what's wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||08-26-2000||10:51 PM||||That's exactly what I was thinking--bad thermostat.  I just wanted to make sure so I don't wreck my engine.  That'd ruin my day.  I'll give it a try and get back to you.  Thanks a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fropuff||08-26-2000||11:58 PM||||I tried the thermo. in water method.  The thermostat opened at 200 degrees (F).  Must be a 195 thermostat.  But, when I looked at it after removal, I saw that one of the "arms" is very bent.  I also noticed that the rubber gasket, or seal, was out of place, like it was pushed up when I put it in; maybe it was too tight a space and made it move.  Should I try putting it back in to see if it will work; maybe it was stuck in the closed position.  Oh, is there a special way to put it in?  I just pushed down, turning it a little, till I couldn't push any more and when I heard a "click".  Thanks a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mdjoneses||08-27-2000||12:58 AM||||My stock thermostate worked well.  Then a garage replaced it and it did the same thing yours did. The rubber seal kept pushing up and the long sides would get bent like yours. The next one I got did the same. Then I bought one at a NAPA store. That worked great.    Mark86se2.8||||reg||10
Z||000005||fiero56||08-27-2000||01:05 AM||||It's pretty difficult to install a thermostat incorrectly.<BR>I would check the oil, and make sure it still looks good, correct level, doesn't look or smell different, no water dropplet looking bubbles, etc.<BR>Also take a look at the coolant. Same procedure, no oil in it, no funny color or smell.<P>Leave thermostat out for this test.<BR>Start the engine(watch oil pressure carefully), and let it run for a minute or so. While the engine is running, touch the 'stat cap, is it warm? Feel the coolant pipe under the passenger door. Does it get warm too? Then feel the the upper hose coming out of the radiator. It should get warm after several minutes too. Then the pipe under the drivers door.<P>Since the 'stat opened, I wonder if the cooling system has a clog in it. Don't let the car overheat! If you have a clog in the system, it is possible the temp guage will not display engine temp. Very scary things can happen when an engine overheats, warping heads, block, etc. Watch that oil pressure while doing this test! It sounds like you overheated the engine already, and its possible your leakign your oil into your coolant or cylinders due to warped heads or block. But this is speculation at this point.<P>I would try another thermostat since this one has some damage to it. Never hurts. At the bottom of the 'stat is a copper looking "plug". On the bottom of it, should be stamped in little numbers "195", this is the temp rating. Stock is 195. <p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 08-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||08-27-2000||07:09 AM||||For easier installation of the 'stat just wipe a touch of vasoline around the "O" ring||||reg||1
Z||000007||fropuff||08-27-2000||09:53 AM||||Okay, I just did the test Fiero56 told me to do.  I let it run for about 3 minutes.  First, I felt the thermostat cap; it was getting warm.  Then, I felt the passenger side coolant pipe; that also got warm.  I think I found the pipe coming out of the radiator, but tried all the pipes and they were warm as well.  Finally, I tried the driver's side coolant pipe, and that was warm.  As the car was idling, it held a constant oil pressure of around 60.  The RPM's fluctuated, but that's normal for a 2.8, right?  It didn't change that much, about from 850-1050.  Okay, now that I have that done, what do I do now?  Thanks a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Xtreme Fiero's||08-27-2000||10:37 AM||||Check your belts, also try running the car with out th thermostat.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fropuff||08-27-2000||11:51 AM||||I ran it without the thermostat in the test I did; the one Fiero56 told me to do.  When you say to check the belts, which ones?  What will that tell me; what should I look for?  THANKS.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||08-27-2000||12:07 PM||||One of the belts runs the water pump. But if they are not squealing, and the coolant was circulating(pipes around the car warmed up)I am going to assume the thermostat was to blame. <BR>Low oil pressure when you pulled in the driveway could have been caused by the oil being super heated, and super thin, since it sounds like the engine was way above operating temp. Try a new thermostat, and see if the problem comes back. Not sure if the oil should be changed or not since it sounds like it got really hot, others might be better with that.<P><BR>I would keep an eye on the oil level, condition, water type droplets, etc. Same for the coolant. I would keep a close eye on oil for about a week(every day), and check the coolant a couple times too. Also keep an eye on the exhaust, no blue smoke, or white smoke. Those can be caused by an overheat condtion. Blue would mean oil, white would mean coolant.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 08-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||fropuff||08-27-2000||12:20 PM||||Okay, I just tested it with the thermostat in.  I let it run for about ten minutes, and the thermostat cap was hot, but not the coolant rails or the line running from the radiator.  The oil pressure also maintained itself, about 40-60.  The oil and coolant levels were normal, and the appearance was fine.  Was it just a faulty thermostat?  It worked before, just not yesterday.  It was functional when I put it in the water, and now the engine doesn't run as hot.  The top of the block didn't get as hot either.  Nor did the rear deck lid.  The vents were hot, but I'm assuming this is normal when I was idleing and not moving.  The hot air usually gets blown out and cooled down when in motion.  Is my engine running correctly now?  I hope so.  I left it in the garage and I'm not going to drive it again until I'm sure that it's all right.  Oh yeah, the belts didn't squeal or squeak either.  It sounded like it was running as good as before.  The idle was slightly eratic, about 900-1000, but it was before as well.  Am I good?  Thanks a lot for all the help, guys.  I'm glad I asked to see what was wrong with it.  I wouldn't have done all the tests and looked for all the things, either.  Thanks again.<P>PS: Mdjoneses:  The thermostat is a NAPA brand too. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||08-27-2000||12:31 PM||||If you let the engine run for ten minutes, that should be more than enough time for the thermostat to open, and allow coolant to flow in the pipes around the car.<BR>Buy a new thermostat. Your current one has some damage to it(bent arm), and when installed might be blocking the valve from opening. If it is your only car, take the 'stat out and drive without one to the autoparts store. Watch temp though, an engine can overheat even without a thermostat.<BR>Thermostats are a little funny about operation. They actually open at different temps under different coolant pressures. Even though yours opened in the pot, I would try another one out. I have always bought thermostats by "stant" and never had any trouble with them.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fropuff||08-29-2000||09:23 PM||||Okay, so I've watched my Fiero for about a few days now.  I had it parked in the garage the entire time, and I didn't notice any leaks or drips.  But, I was wondering:  When the cooling system is operating correctly, are the cooling pipes and radiator hoses supposed to be warm?  They are.  Thanks a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000014||fiero56||08-29-2000||09:35 PM||||The coolant pipes and hoses should be warm, even hot, whenever the car has run for at least a few minutes||||reg||1
Z||000015||rprevo||08-30-2000||08:05 AM|||| It might be a good idea to see if the<BR>Radiator fan was coming on. <P>Ray||||reg||1
Z||000016||Carrolles||08-30-2000||08:15 AM||||You might look underneath the car to see that the stainless steel coolant pipes that run along the rocker panels are not crimped or crushed anywhere.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000017||fropuff||09-02-2000||11:00 AM||||Well, it did the same thing again!  Took it for the nightly 10-15 mile drive, and when I got home, it stalled.  While I was driving it, I saw that the temp. gauge was reading about 190-200.  When I was at a stop sign, I opened the door and felt the coolant rail.  It was hot.  So, I continued on.  The entire time I was driving, the oil pressure was about 40-50 when idling.  So, when I got home, I let my dad off at the front door, and the oil pressure light came on (the gauge was at ZERO) and it stalled.  Instead of starting it again, we just pushed it in the garage for the night.  The top of the block, the vents, and the thermostat cap were hot, but not as bad as before.  I looked under the car, and none of the cooling components are crimped or bent.  Everything got hot when it was running, and the gauge worked fine. (I just put in another "new" thermostat.)  I noticed that the radiator fan came on when I had run it before.  Everything I looked for seemed to be working just fine.  I'm not going to take it out again till I find what the problem is.  I am going to have my dad bring it to the mechanic on Tuesday if I can't find what's wrong.  I really need your help.  Can you help me???  Thanks a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000018||StuGood||09-02-2000||01:12 PM||||In the very first post, you said that you "noticed that my new theremostat wasn't reading."  By that I assume you meant the temp. gage wasn't giving a reading?<P>Think everyone is focusing on the thermostat at this point, because you said you changed it only "25 miles ago."  I think I agree with them.<P>When the thermostat was changed, did you also drain / replace the coolant?  If so, you might want to try "burping" any air out of the cooling system.  I've had scary-looking temperature readings show on the gage when I failed to get all of the air bubbles out.  That would by <B>my</B> next move.<P>FWIW, you mentioned one of the thermostat's  "arms" was bent, and you asked if there were any "tricks" to installing the 'stat.  Here're my opinions:<P>I think the "bail" on the thermostat has to be there - the cap presses down on the bail and holds the thermostat in place.  I've also read (in this forum) that running around without a 'stat can actually <B>cause</B> the engine to overheat.  I can't figure out why that would be (yet), but I tend to believe it's true.<P>A collapsed bail on a thermostat can be carefully straightened with pliers, and the thermostat reused, if it's otherwise functional.<P>Whenever I install a "bail" type thermostat (such as on a Fiero), place it in the  housing by hand, then use a long screwdriver to gently press the <B>base</B> of the 'stat into position.  If I try to press it all the way into place by hand, I risk collapsing the bail.<P>Sorry so long - hope this helps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000019||fropuff||09-02-2000||02:26 PM||||That helped a lot.  I did not change the coolant when I changed the thermostat.  Should I do this?  I noticed that the coolant is a LITTLE low, but not much.  The new thermostat is functional, as I tested it in the boiling water.  The "arms" didn't bend as much this time.  It seems the "arms" are a little too long, and I think the cap, when turned, made the "arm" bend.  Should I try changing the coolant?  How about the oil?  Is the oil screwed up mow that it possibly overheated?  Thanks a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000020||StuGood||09-02-2000||02:42 PM||||The "arms" shouldn't bend at <B>all</B>, when you install the thermostat.  Be sure to press the base of the 'stat with the screwdriver at various points around its periphery, a little at a time, until it's fully seated.  You may have to make several rounds.<P>You'll know when it's seated because the end of the "bail" won't be sticking above the top of the water outlet.  That way, the bail won't collapse when you put the cap on.<P>Changing oil never hurts.  Well, it never hurts the <B>engine</B>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> .<BR>Changing coolant never hurts anything - as <B>long</B> as you burp all the air out.<P>Oops.  I just generalized.  Twice.  Never do that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  <P>How did all this start, again?  Did you say the temperature gage wouldn't give a reading?     ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Patrick||09-02-2000||02:47 PM||||<BR>Unless you bought the wrong thermostat, there's no way that the "arms" would be too long. If your cap is pushing down on them as you're turning it, then I'd say you haven't got the thermostat pushed down far enough. <P>I had a problem installing my last thermostat, it also didn't want to go down far enough. I used some emery cloth on the bottom of the inside of the thermostat housing to remove a little build-up of crap/corrosion. A little grease on the seal helped it pop into position also.<P>I think what's happened is that because you have forced the cap on while the last TWO thermostats have NOT been pushed down far enough, that you have caused both thermostats to bind (and not open) because of the stress on the "arms".||||reg||1
Z||000022||Patrick||09-02-2000||02:50 PM||||<BR>Hey <B>Stu</B>, great minds must think alike. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Your post appeared while I was typing mine out.||||reg||14
Z||000023||fropuff||09-02-2000||02:51 PM||||Thanks again.  Yes, it started when I noticed that the temp. gauge, not the thermostat, was not reading.  I didn't really think anything of it, thought the car wasn't hot enough by then.  Then I found out that the car was running VERY hot.  It went on from there.  I might try the method to get it in there right.  I might also change the oil, filter, and coolant.  How to I "burp" out the air, as you said it?  I'll get back to you.  Thanks again.  ||||reg||1
Z||000024||fropuff||09-02-2000||02:53 PM||||Also, how am I to drain the coolant?  Don't want to drain anything else, like the fuel or refrigerant or anything like that.  And, can anyone give me a step-by-step process of how to drain, change, etc.?  Need to be sure.  Thanks.<p>[This message has been edited by fropuff (edited 09-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||StuGood||09-02-2000||03:13 PM||||If your luck works like mine, you really don't want to try to do too many things at once.  Start with installing the thermostat properly, and getting the air out.  When you get the overheating problem fixed, change the oil, etc.<P>To get the air out:<P>Step One: With the car cold, and thermostat out, remove (front) radiator cap and leave it off.  Optional:  raise rear of car slightly.<P>Step two: Add coolant at the thermostat housing until it runs out of the front radiator cap.  Install front radiator cap.  <P>Step three:  Continue to add coolant at the 'stat housing until it's pretty much full).  Lower car if necessary, and add coolant to coolant bottle in front compartment, if it needs any.  Install thermostat and 'stat cap.      <P>Step four:  Test drive, see if the overheating is cured.  Let engine warm up, then let it cool down so it can draw coolant from the bottle if it needs to.  Keep an eye on the coolant level in the bottle.  <P> <BR>Patrick:  Didn't mean to step on your toes.  This guy will think we have great minds, until he finds out it was the water pump    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> !    <P><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 09-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||fropuff||09-02-2000||03:41 PM||||.||||reg||1
Z||000027||JohnnyK||09-02-2000||07:09 PM||||My two vents are way to hot to touch after driving for a few miles, but my temp guage never goes past the first (1/4) mark (well, it does sometimes, but rarely). Is this odd?||||reg||1
Z||000028||fiero56||09-02-2000||09:53 PM||||To burp the cooling system, go here: <A HREF="http://www.surf.to/FIERO_OSG" TARGET=_blank>http://www.surf.to/FIERO_OSG</A> <P>Then click on "cooling system", then "bleeding the cooling system". Should help.<P>Is your oil pressure needle steady, or does it rapidly bounce around? Having another brain storm here.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000029||fropuff||09-02-2000||10:23 PM||||Thanks for the help guys.  I attempted to "burp" the system as StuGood told me.  I did everything I needed to; added some when needed.  Everything worked out fine.  Then, I took it for a drive (about 2 miles) to let it warm up.  Didn't seem like anything was wrong.  After a while, we took it for a 10-15 mile drive, in which it did the SAME THING!  The temp. gauge read about 190.  The pressure also dropped to twenty when I was stopped at a sign.  When I'm driving and when I first start it, it's usually at 40-50.  It also got a little hot, but I didn't let it overheat.  The coolant from in the bottle overflowed, but that's becuase I added it when it was cold.  Everything seemed to work fine, and now I'm guessing it has nothing to do with the 'stat.  ||||reg||1
Z||000030||fiero56||09-02-2000||11:55 PM||||I think I know the culprit. I can't believe I didn't think of it befire. Replace your radiator cap with a 16lb release non-vented cap.<P>Is this the first time your coolant bottle has overflowed? <P>Also, is your oil pressure needle steady, or does it rapidly change readings?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 09-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||fropuff||09-03-2000||12:04 AM||||Yes, it was the first time it overflowed.  The only reason it did was because I filled it too full, and it also was cold.  It overflowed from the plastic bottle where you can see the coolant level.  Should I still get a new cap?  Radiator cap or thermostat housing cap?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000032||fiero56||09-03-2000||12:14 AM||||I had a similar problem about a year ago. Anytime I drove the car for a good 15 minutes or longer, my Fiero would all of a sudden begin to overheat rapidly. I traced it down to a bad radiator cap that was allowing the coolant to exit the system into the overflow bottle, and would occasionally overfill the bottle. Caps are pretty cheap, and anything is cheaper than a mechanic.<P>I just re-read the entire thread again. How long have you owned/driven this car? Kinda' sounds like you just got it. If so, I think I know whats up.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 09-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000033||fropuff||09-03-2000||12:19 AM||||Oh.  So is it the radiator cap or the thermostat housing cap?  Thanks.  ||||reg||1
Z||000034||fiero56||09-03-2000||12:23 AM||||Replace the radiator cap.||||reg||1
Z||000035||fropuff||09-03-2000||12:25 AM||||Okay, great.  I'll try to get one tomorrow if the auto parts store is open.  Thanks a lot for the help.  I'll try it and get back to you.  Thanks again everyone!!!||||reg||1
Z||000036||fropuff||09-03-2000||12:28 AM||||By the way:  I did just get it.  If you consider April just getting it, sure.  I;ve probably only put 250 miles on it since then, however.  ||||reg||1
Z||000037||fiero56||09-03-2000||12:33 AM||||Has it always overheated since you got it, or is this a new(er) problem?||||reg||1
Z||000038||fropuff||09-03-2000||12:35 AM||||It had never overheated before.  The temp. gauge never worked, so I just decided to change the 'stat.  The problems started after that.  ||||reg||1
Z||000039||fropuff||09-04-2000||12:27 PM||||Fiero56:  Should I still try to get a different radiator cap?  I took the thermostat out yesterday and took it for a 15 mile test drive.  It did get hot, though the temperature gauge only read 130-150.  I did have the A/C on though.  The oil pressure remained at 30-50, even at stop signs.  When I pulled in, it read about 25.  It did get hot, about as same as the first time.  What should I do now?  Get a new cap?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000040||StuGood||09-04-2000||02:33 PM||||You don't need to get a new cap - the one you're wearing on your head looks fine      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>Seriously (just for a change of pace), I don't think you ever answered rprevo's question about whether the radiator fan was coming on.  Does the radiator fan come on when you turn the A/C on (it should)?  By the ways, sounds as if your (new?) temp. gage is working now - ? <P>Next thing I might try, is ground the green/white wire to the temp. switch, so that the radiator fan stays on whenever the ignition is turned on.  It costs nothing, and it's worth a try! <P>I don't suppose any of the rubber radiator hoses (two in front that connect to the radiator, plus the one on each side of the car that connect to the pipes), are kinked?  There is also a metal pipe that runs from the RHS to LHS of the car, between the engine and firewall (forward of engine).  Might check those out for kinks/leaks.<P>Did your car ingest a newspaper/ plastic bag that's blocking the radiator?  Can you tell I'm running out of ideas?               <P>Might try reading out trouble codes, and checking/adjusting the ignition timing.  After that, I guess it's "hidden" circulation problems, like a partially-clogged radiator.  Changing coolant might provide clues - you wanted to do that, anyway, I think.  <P><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 09-04-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000041||fropuff||09-04-2000||05:11 PM||||Yes, the fan came on when I turn the A/C on.  The temperature gauge also works correctly.  I'll look again closely for a kink, but I couldn't find one the first time.  I might have my dad bring it to the mechanic tomorrow to have them see what's going on.  I was watching Horsepower TV yesterday on TNN, and they were screwing around with the cooling system.  The added a new radiator and something else.  They said that the car was running hot.  They did this, got a new thermostat and pipes that don't collapse.  They said if this doesn't fix the hot-running, it may be that the timing is off, a gasket is blown, or a screwed up water pump.  It might also be one of these.  I'm not sure.  I'm sure that the mechanic will be able to find out.  Should I do this?  I really want to find out.  Thanks.  ||||reg||1
Z||000042||fiero56||09-04-2000||05:43 PM||||I'm running very low on ideas. I asked about how long ago you had bought the car, thinking maybe someone decided to unload the car, since a gasket, or a head, or the block, was bad. It is possible that at least one of these is bad, especially since it sounds like you overheated the engine. This possiblility grows tremendously when an engine is overheated.<P>Like the others said, check timing. That's my last idea. If the timing is on, and a new cap doesn't do the trick, I will have to assume that something $$$ is bad.||||reg||1
Z||000043||fropuff||11-25-2000||02:55 PM||||Well, today it didn't just get hot, it overheated.  I had it running at idle for about 20 minutes.  I went over to my grandpa's house to air up the tires.  When I was pulling it into the garage, I noticed steam coming from under the front hood.  I also noticed that the temperature gauge read was pegged at 260.  A few minutes before, it had read around 200.  So I quickly shut it off, and I heard that the coolant was overflowing.  I waited a few minutes and opened the lid.  The overflow bottle is empty, there's coolant all over the place, as well as on the road and all the way down the driveway.  I opened up the rear deck lid, and took the cap off.  The thermostat was working fine; it was still opened in fact.  The entire cooling system seems to be drained a lot of the coolant.  I didn't have the fan on, but I don't remember hearing it on, even when the gauge read 260.  What should I do now?  Oh, by the way, the engine wasn't hot this time.  A little warm, about usual.  Thanks for any help.    <P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000044||fropuff||11-25-2000||03:06 PM||||.||||reg||1
