Z||000058||Indiana_resto_guy||02-26-2001||10:20 PM||||After all this is going on and for the time period that is involved, I think that I would remove the radiator and get it boiled out. <BR>Sounds like it's clogged up.<BR>Some one asked eailer in the post about running without a thermostat, the car will overheat without one installed. It will do so because nothing slows the flow down enough for the radiator to cool the fluid enough before it is reintroduced to the engine.<BR>This would also be a good time to flush the ENTIRE cooling system.||||reg||1
Z||000059||fropuff||02-26-2001||10:27 PM||||Thanks for the reply.  How do I go about cleaning the entire system?  What I mean is, what do I need to do, other than draining out all the coolant.  Also, where do I bring the radiator to get it "boiled out"?  Do I need to disassemble anything else when I clean out the system?  I will first try the caps that I will buy tomorrow, and if they don't work, I'll clean the system.  Thanks again for any help you can give. <P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||5
Z||000060||Indiana_resto_guy||02-27-2001||12:35 AM||||Remove it (the radiator) from the car and take it to a radiator repair shop. They can clean it out if it needs it done. They should also be able to tell you the best way to flush your system out.||||reg||7
Z||000061||Cliff Pennock||02-27-2001||05:36 AM||||Does the <B>Check Engine</B> light come on at all? I find it weird that you haven't mentioned that yet, so I suspect the bulb has been removed. If you turn your ignition to the <B>ON</B> position, does the light switch on too? I'm pretty sure the ECM has set an error code, but you are simply not seeing it.<P>I also suspect a malfunctioning waterpump, allthough I have no idea how to check that.||||reg||1
Z||000062||ThatRickGuy||02-27-2001||09:00 AM||||Okay, well here's my logic on your heat problem.<P>Coolant appears to be flowing, as in all the pipes warm up correctly. but just to be sure, pull the thermostat, leave the cover off and start the car, look down the thermostat neck and see if the coolant is flowing. If it is, to me that would point away from the bad waterpump/clogged radiator.<P>so, from there I would put a new 16lbs non-vented cap on the radiator.<P>make sure you burp the system, this can take a few attempts to get it completely clear.<P>Also, check the temp sensor that controls the fan, it should go on at 220 degrees, if it doesn't, replace it, there's 3 different temp guages on the engine, and I can't remember which one the Fan Switch is, but someone here will know.<P>You mentioned being in school, wether it's HS or college, it means you're probrably not on the high end of the pay scale(Atleast, I never had cash while I was in school), so I would say try these little things as the parts are much cheaper the paying a shop to do anything.||||reg||1
Z||000063||87GTZ34||02-27-2001||12:02 PM||||The fan temp sensor is the one that comes out directly from below the fill/thermostat housing. It is a double pole thermistor. To test all but the sensor/switch, warmup eng, shut-off car, pull connector on thermistor, turn ign switch on (not started though) and then take the wires off the fan thermistor and use a wire to complete the circuit on the connector.  The fan should kick on. Shut off ign before reconnecting.<BR>To clean the system you should:<BR>1) fix all leaks and get both caps working.<BR>   to do this I bought another thermostat hsng (in good shape), get it boiled out at machine shop straighten the bottom and the cap surface, then file both flat (don't over do it) repaint it with heat tolerant paint and re-install it w/ new gasket & high temp sealer.<BR>2) when cool, drain all fluid from bottom tubes (and the motor side drains if you are picky like me WARNING - this is a pain in the a$$).  put all plugs in, put in 1 container of coolant flush(prestone or whatever), refill with distilled water (and buy lots of D-water), burp system then put thermostat back in and run per directions on flush (usually 10 min).  Let cool drain all fluid again and refill w/ more D-water.  I rinsed the flush 3 times on mine.  After draining the last rinse, refill with 50/50 coolant and D-water.  I used the GM approved Havoline DEX-COOL for longer coolant life.  This tightens the cooling system up very well.  IMHO, I would take the above advice and bring your radiator to a shop have it cleaned and resealed before flushing.  If it is in rough shape (inside), I would also bring the heater core as it is most likely in rough shape too (same coolant). Inspect all hoses and replace if soft/spongy or cracked as this is not a fun thing to do very often.<P>most of all, have fun, be patient and enjoy your fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000064||fropuff||02-27-2001||06:27 PM||||Well, I don't know if this means that the water pump operates correctly, but as soon as I turned the car on with the stat out and the cap off, coolant overflowed out the housing.  When I turned the car off, it stopped doing this and the level was obviously down.  I also put a new radiator cap on (non-vented 16-lbs.), but I'm going to wait until I get the housing cap.  Could you please tell me if this means that the water pump is working right?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000065||fropuff||02-27-2001||06:45 PM||||I just realized something...when I changed the coolant, I did not use distilled water.  I was looking at a website on how to boil out a radiator, and it says that regular tap water contains minerals that can cause crystals and they may collect other crap and cause a clog.  Should I drain out all the coolant and fill it up with a 50/50 coolant/water mixture?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000066||87GTZ34||02-27-2001||08:14 PM||||I wouldn't worry about that until you have the root cause fixed and are going to put the 'final' coolant in it.  What you described sounds like the radiator may be clogged as the coolant wouldn't flow down the tube to the radiator.  That could also be caused by back pressure anywhere to the engine coolant intake side.  Your water pump is circulating with no pressure okay but that could change with the system closed up (pressurized).  I would assume the water pump is okay for now and have the radiator checked.  For a quick test, take the return side (lower) hose off and put water in the radiator.  It should flow out as quickly as you can put it in.  If it backs up, it definitely needs to be cleaned.||||reg||1
